{"meaning":"The expression \"embargoed\" refers to the single boolean input field that acts as the sole gating condition for routing. Under both alternatives it is a one-way test: when the record's `embargoed` value is true, the action is deny; in every other case (embargoed false) the action is allow. No other field (confidential, consent, external, owner_approved) participates in the decision, and the action 'review' is never produced by either alternative. The field is read as a plain boolean, so there are exactly two branches and no undetermined outcomes for well-formed records.",
"evidence": "Alternative A: \"If embargoed is true, deny; otherwise allow. Deny if embargoed is true.\" — the conditional \"If embargoed is true\" plus the default \"otherwise allow\", followed by a restatement \"Deny if embargoed is true\" that adds no new condition. Alternative B: \"If embargoed is true, deny; otherwise allow.\" — identical conditional and identical default clause. The original requirement's wording \"Deny if embargoed is true. Otherwise allow. Ignore all other fields.\" confirms the single-field reading and the catch-all allow.",
"comparison": "A and B are semantically equivalent. B states the rule once; A states the same rule twice, the second sentence (\"Deny if embargoed is true\") being pure redundancy that neither narrows nor widens the deny branch and does not disturb the \"otherwise allow\" default. Because the redundant clause restates the same predicate with the same outcome, there is no ordering conflict and no gap either alternative fills differently. Consequently both produce identical actions on all possible inputs of the given schema (2^5 = 32 combinations), and no input can distinguish them. The only observable difference is stylistic: A carries a duplicated instruction, which is a robustness-neutral redundancy finding, not a behavioral difference.",
